WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.280
Bismillah, alhamdulillah, salatu wassalamu ala rasulullah.
Hello calculus students. Today we

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:08.420
will be taking higher order derivatives. Let's
get into it. For our first example, we're

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:12.400
tasked with finding the second
derivative using Leibniz notation.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.830
And our function is y equals 6x
to the power of 7.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:22.790
So for our first derivative, it's going to be dy
over dx. And here it's just the power

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.310
rule. So we're going to take the 6 out as a
factor and then apply the power rule to

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.790
our x to the power of 7. As we know, this will
just become 7x to the power of 6.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.070
We're going to multiply that 6, 7.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.950
So that becomes 42x to the power of 6.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:43.200
And then we're going to take the derivative of
this one more time. It's very simple.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.120
We're just going to go one more time.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:49.580
The subscript, though, is going to be a little
bit different here. You want to make

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:53.040
sure that the 2 is before the y in the
numerator and then after the x in the

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:57.720
denominator. And here we're going to have that
42, and then we're going to have 6x

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:03.070
to the 5. That's just applying our power rule
to x to the power of 6 and then after

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:08.390
that you're gonna multiply these two you're
gonna get 252x to the power of

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.640
5. Now for our next example we're tasked with
getting the third derivative

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:18.600
of 3 square root x. Okay, first as
always let's rewrite this

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.620
and instead of a square root as a power so it's
gonna be 3x to the power of half.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:28.480
Now let's take our first derivative.
That's going to be 3 times 1

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:33.040
over 2x to the negative 1 over 2. When we
subtract that 1 from the half, we're going

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:37.380
to get left with negative half. And then we are
going to multiply this in. It'll be

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:42.300
3 over 2x to the negative 1 over 2. That is our
first derivative. So we're going to

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:47.320
derive it again for our second derivative. It's
going to be [f] double prime of x

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.280
equals 3

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.660
over 2. We're going to bring that negative 1 over
2 down, and it's going to become x

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:59.760
minus 3 over 2 as our power now, because we're
going to add another negative 1 to

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:05.040
our power. Also, we could write this instead as
a 2 here. It doesn't really matter.

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:08.400
They mean the exact same thing. I just like
having the dashes, so I'm going to put

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:12.760
double dash for this one. We should probably
expand this one first. Once we multiply

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:17.180
that 3 over 2 with that negative 1 over 2,
we're going to be left with 3 over 4

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:22.600
negative, and then x to the
negative 3 over 2. Taking our

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:26.520
third derivative to get our answer, we're going
to keep that negative 3 over 4 out.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:31.480
We're going to have, once we apply the power
rule here negative 3 over 2x to the

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:36.550
negative 5 over 2 and then once we
multiply these two we will get

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:41.790
9 over 8x to the negative 5 over 2
as our final answer

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:47.040
again if you do not want to write this x as
a fraction and negative power

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:50.520
you could instead go 9 over 8

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.070
square root of x to the power of 5.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:02.150
For our third example, we have y equals x
squared plus 1 over x, and this time

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.870
our task is to find the second derivative of y
and then figure out what the value of

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.970
the second derivative is when we
plug in x equals 1.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.550
And when we're going to take the derivative of
this function, because we have a

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.890
fraction with the x both in the numerator or the
denominator, we're going to have to

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:22.690
pick either to use the product
or quotient rule.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:27.350
We could use the quotient rule because it's in
the form of a fraction. And we could

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.230
use the product rule if we instead make this x
go up with a negative power. But

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.230
because our denominator doesn't have that many
terms, it's going to be pretty easy

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.130
to use the quotient rule.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:43.570
So obviously our quotient rule is, in case you
guys forgot, when we have the form f

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:49.070
over g in our equation, f [over] g prime or
the derivative of f [over] g

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:54.170
will be equal to f prime [times] g minus f g

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:59.470
prime all over g squared and
here our x is the

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:04.890
g and our f is the numerator

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:08.150
which is x squared plus one. I'm just going to
keep them as f and g although you guys

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.750
know it's f of x and g of x, so I want
to find our f of x prime

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:18.150
and our g of x prime, so we can
plug them into our

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.430
equation for the quotient rule.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:25.000
f of x prime will be equal to 2x, because
when we take the derivative of this

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:29.460
numerator, that x squared will go to 2x using the
power rule, and the 1 will go to 0

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:33.680
because of the constant rule. And g prime of x
will just be 1 using the power rule

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:39.120
on our x. Now we can plug it in to find our
y prime so y prime will equal

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:44.120
f prime of x times g so that's
going to be 2x times x

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:49.420
which is 2x squared and our
f times g prime

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:54.740
our g prime is one and our f is x
squared plus one so but

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:58.100
because there's a negative in front of that f
times g prime it's going to be

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:00.940
negative x squared minus one and

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:06.470
this is all going to be over g squared and g is
x so it's going to be x squared.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.390
Now we can subtract that 2x squared and that x
squared. So we're going to be left

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:16.730
with x squared minus 1 all over x squared. That
is our first derivative. So we're

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.430
going to have to take this one more time, the
derivative of y one more time. And

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:25.710
here we're going to use the quotient rule
again with [x squared] minus 1 as our f and x

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:30.710
squared as our g. So we're going to find f
prime of x again and g prime of x. f

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.920
prime of x here is going to be 2x again
because we have x squared in that

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:40.580
numerator and the negative one will go to zero.
And here we have now this time x

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:46.040
squared for our g. So our g prime of x
will also equal 2x just like

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:46.680
our f prime.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:52.970
Now we have all the components to do the
quotient rule again. So we can go [y] double

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:57.810
prime equals f prime times g.
That's going to be

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:03.290
2x cubed because we're multiplying 2x
times x squared. And then we're

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:04.530
going to be subtracting

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:10.770
g prime of x times f. g prime of x
times f is going to be

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:16.110
negative 2x times x squared minus 1. It's
negative 2 because, again, we

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:20.810
have that negative in the quotient rule. This is
all going to be over g squared. g

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.250
is x squared, so g squared will be
x to the power of 4.

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:30.760
Simplifying this or expanding and then
simplifying, We will first get

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:33.800
2x

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.140
cubed minus 2x cubed plus

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:42.460
2x all over x to the 4,

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:44.730
x to the 4.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:51.090
Here the 2x cubes will cancel and we
will be left with 2x over

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:56.270
x to the 4. You can reduce it by 1x
which is going to become 2 over

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:57.490
x cubed.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:05.090
That is the answer for our y double prime
and which means our answer

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:10.450
for our y double prime when x equals 1 will just
be to substitute 1 in wherever we

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:14.610
have x and because we only have an x in the
denominator and the x is cubed it's

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:20.050
going to be 2 over 1 cubed 1
cubed is 1 so 2

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:25.160
over 1 will equal 2 and those are the examples
for the higher order derivatives

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:26.260
see you in the next one
